    "## Q1. Sensor Readings\n",
    "Given temperature readings with an average measurement error of +2°C."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "id": "5a604cf9",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "import numpy as np\n",
    "\n",
    "temperature = np.array([25, 28, 31, 35, 38, 27, 33, 40])\n",
    "\n",
    "# a. Add 2°C to every reading using vectorization\n",
    "corrected_temperature = temperature + 2\n",
    "print(\"Corrected temperatures (°C):\", corrected_temperature)\n",
    "\n",
    "# b. Convert Celsius to Fahrenheit\n",
    "fahrenheit = (9/5) * corrected_temperature + 32\n",
    "print(\"Temperatures (°F):\", fahrenheit)\n",
    "\n",
    "# c. Identify readings greater than 32°C using Boolean indexing\n",
    "greater_than_32 = corrected_temperature[corrected_temperature > 32]\n",
    "print(\"Readings greater than 32°C:\", greater_than_32)\n",
    "\n",
    "# d. Count how many readings exceed 32°C\n",
    "count = np.sum(corrected_temperature > 32)\n",
    "print(\"Number of readings exceeding 32°C:\", count)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "7190aea5",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### e. Explanation\n",
    "**Vectorization** performs an operation on an entire NumPy array at once instead of explicitly iterating through every element with a `for` loop. This is generally faster and makes the code shorter and easier to read.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Boolean indexing** creates a Boolean mask such as `temperature > 32` and directly selects the values satisfying the condition. This avoids manually checking each reading and is efficient for large datasets."
   ]
  },
